        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A pulse oximeter is a non-invasive medical device that estimates the percentage of haemoglobin in arterial blood that is saturated with oxygen, expressed as peripheral oxygen saturation (SpO₂). It also displays the pulse rate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It uses light absorption to estimate oxygen saturation without requiring an arterial blood sample."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Typical OSCE instruction: “Identify this instrument, explain its principle, demonstrate its use, state the normal readings, and discuss its limitations.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Types of pulse oximeters",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Fingertip pulse oximeter"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clips onto a finger and displays SpO₂ and pulse rate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Routine adult observations, outpatient assessment and selected home monitoring."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Bedside pulse oximeter"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often integrated into a multiparameter monitor with continuous or intermittent monitoring."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Emergency departments, operating theatres, intensive care and inpatient monitoring."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Pediatric and neonatal pulse oximeter"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses appropriately sized sensors for small fingers, hands or feet."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Monitoring children and newborns, including neonatal resuscitation and assessment for congenital heart disease."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Light-emitting diodes (LEDs)",
                      "Emit red and infrared light."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Photodetector",
                      "Measures the light reaching the detector."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Probe or sensor",
                      "Positions the light source and detector around the tissue."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Display",
                      "Shows SpO₂ and pulse rate; some devices also show a plethysmographic waveform."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Alarm system",
                      "Alerts staff to configured thresholds or technical problems on equipped monitors."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cable or power source",
                      "Connects the sensor and powers the device."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pulse oximetry relies on differences in light absorption by oxygenated and deoxygenated haemoglobin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Oxygenated haemoglobin absorbs relatively more infrared light.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Deoxygenated haemoglobin absorbs relatively more red light.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The device analyzes changes in transmitted or reflected light associated with pulsatile arterial blood flow.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It uses these signals to estimate SpO₂."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important distinction: Pulse oximetry estimates oxygen saturation; it does not directly measure arterial oxygen tension (PaO₂), carbon dioxide tension (PaCO₂), or haemoglobin concentration."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing oxygenation in patients with respiratory distress.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring patients with pneumonia, asthma, COPD and other respiratory disorders.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring oxygenation during anaesthesia, sedation and recovery.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing critically ill patients.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring patients receiving supplemental oxygen.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Evaluating children with respiratory illness.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Supporting neonatal resuscitation and monitoring.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Screening newborns for critical congenital heart disease using an appropriate protocol.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring patients during transport and selected procedures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "9."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A pulse oximeter is an adjunct to clinical assessment. It must not delay emergency treatment when the patient is visibly deteriorating."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Normal and abnormal SpO₂ readings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For most healthy adults at sea level, SpO₂ is generally 95–100%. Interpretation depends on the patient's condition, baseline saturation, altitude, and prescribed oxygen targets."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "SpO₂ reading",
                    "General interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "95–100%",
                      "Usually normal at sea level"
                    ],
                    [
                      "92–94%",
                      "May be below expected; assess the patient and clinical context"
                    ],
                    [
                      "90–91%",
                      "Concerning hypoxaemia; prompt clinical assessment is required"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Below 90%",
                      "Significant hypoxaemia is likely; urgent assessment and management are generally required"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are general guideposts, not universal treatment thresholds. Patients with certain chronic respiratory conditions may have individualized oxygen targets, commonly 88–92% in those at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure when clinically appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE safety point: A normal SpO₂ does not exclude respiratory failure, carbon dioxide retention, severe anaemia, or other serious illness."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Non-invasive and painless.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provides rapid estimates of oxygen saturation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Portable and easy to use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Enables continuous monitoring with suitable equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful in adults, children and neonates with appropriate probes.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Helps assess response to oxygen therapy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can identify falling oxygen saturation before obvious cyanosis develops.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages and limitations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Does not directly measure PaO₂ or PaCO₂.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can be inaccurate with poor peripheral perfusion.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Motion can interfere with the signal.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Nail polish and artificial nails can interfere with some sensors.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Accuracy can be affected by dyshemoglobins, including carboxyhaemoglobin and methaemoglobin.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Skin pigmentation can contribute to measurement bias; some devices may overestimate oxygen saturation in people with darker skin, potentially concealing hypoxaemia.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Accuracy varies by device, sensor, saturation range and clinical circumstances.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A reading may be unreliable if the probe is incorrectly positioned.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. How to use a pulse oximeter: OSCE demonstration",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_11",
              "group_title": "Practical checklist",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "0 of 10 steps completed"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Perform hand hygiene, introduce yourself, confirm the patient's identity, explain the procedure and obtain consent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Assess the patient's general appearance, respiratory effort, colour and level of consciousness."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Select an appropriate, clean sensor for the patient's age and body size."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Choose a suitable site, usually a finger in an adult or an approved hand/foot site in an infant."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Ensure the site is warm and adequately perfused; remove interfering nail products if necessary and practical."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "6. Position the probe correctly, ensuring the light source and detector align as designed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "7. Switch on the device and allow the reading to stabilize."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "8. Check that the displayed pulse rate corresponds reasonably to the patient's actual pulse and inspect the waveform or signal indicator if available."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "9. Record SpO₂, pulse rate, oxygen delivery method, flow or concentration where relevant, and the patient's clinical condition."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "10. If the result is unexpected, assess the patient first, check the probe and signal, and repeat or confirm the measurement as clinically appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**How to report the result**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A good OSCE report includes the saturation, pulse rate, and oxygen support."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Example:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“The patient's SpO₂ is 97% on room air, with a pulse rate of 84 beats per minute. The reading is stable with a satisfactory signal.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Another example:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“The patient's SpO₂ is 88% while receiving supplemental oxygen. This is concerning and requires immediate clinical reassessment and appropriate escalation.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not delay urgent treatment while trying to obtain a perfect reading in a severely unwell patient."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Common causes of inaccurate readings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Cause",
                    "Why it matters"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Cold extremities",
                      "Vasoconstriction reduces the pulsatile signal."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Shock or poor perfusion",
                      "Weak peripheral blood flow can make readings unreliable."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Patient movement",
                      "Produces artefacts that interfere with the signal."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Incorrect probe placement",
                      "Prevents appropriate alignment of the light source and detector."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nail polish or artificial nails",
                      "May interfere with light transmission, depending on the product and device."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bright ambient light",
                      "Can interfere with some sensors."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Carbon monoxide poisoning",
                      "Standard pulse oximeters may report a reassuring saturation despite significant poisoning."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Methaemoglobinaemia",
                      "May cause readings to trend toward approximately 85%, depending on the actual saturation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Skin pigmentation",
                      "Some devices may overestimate saturation, especially at lower oxygen levels."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Severe anaemia",
                      "SpO₂ may appear normal despite reduced total oxygen-carrying capacity."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_13",
              "group_title": "Important examiner question: Why can SpO₂ be normal in severe anaemia?",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "SpO₂ measures the percentage of haemoglobin binding sites occupied by oxygen, not the total amount of haemoglobin in the blood."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A patient can have a saturation of 98% but a low haemoglobin concentration, resulting in reduced arterial oxygen content."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Similarly, a patient with carbon monoxide poisoning can have a falsely reassuring standard pulse oximeter reading. Suspected poisoning requires appropriate assessment and co-oximetry rather than reliance on standard SpO₂."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Contraindications and complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contraindications: There are no major absolute contraindications to routine pulse oximetry. However, the probe should not be applied to an injured, infected, poorly perfused or otherwise unsuitable site if doing so could cause harm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Potential complications and precautions:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Skin irritation or pressure injury from prolonged sensor application.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Thermal injury from some sensors, especially with prolonged use or poor positioning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Misleading reassurance from inaccurate readings.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Unnecessary intervention from artefacts or false low readings.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In neonates and other patients requiring prolonged monitoring, inspect the sensor site regularly and follow manufacturer recommendations for placement and repositioning."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Pediatric and neonatal considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/pulse_oximeter_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use a sensor validated for the child's age and size.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid placing an adult fingertip device on a neonate unless the device and probe are approved for that use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure the sensor is not excessively tight.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check the skin regularly for pressure injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Interpret the reading in conjunction with respiratory effort, heart rate, perfusion and colour.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "During neonatal resuscitation, use the recommended preductal sensor placement on the right hand or wrist when indicated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "For newborn screening for critical congenital heart disease, use the recommended screening protocol, which commonly includes right-hand and foot measurements.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Neonatal OSCE distinction: Preductal and postductal saturations can differ. Their interpretation depends on the clinical context, timing after birth and the applicable neonatal protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Mnemonics and memory tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_16",
              "group_title": "For checking an unexpected reading: SIGNAL",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "S — Sensor correctly positioned.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "I — Inspect the patient first.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "G — Good peripheral perfusion?",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "N — Nail products or other interference?",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A — Assess waveform and pulse agreement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "L — Look again or use an alternative assessment if unreliable.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_17",
              "group_title": "For the key limitation: SpO₂ is not PaO₂",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remember that oxygen saturation and the partial pressure of oxygen are related but different measurements."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pulse_oximeter_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pulse_oximeter_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What does a pulse oximeter measure?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It estimates peripheral arterial oxygen saturation (SpO₂) and usually displays pulse rate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the principle of pulse oximetry?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It uses differences in red and infrared light absorption by oxygenated and deoxygenated haemoglobin, analyzing the pulsatile component of the signal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Does a normal SpO₂ exclude carbon monoxide poisoning?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Standard pulse oximeters cannot reliably distinguish carboxyhaemoglobin from oxyhaemoglobin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the difference between SpO₂ and PaO₂?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "SpO₂ estimates the percentage of haemoglobin binding sites occupied by oxygen. PaO₂ is the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen in arterial blood, measured using an arterial blood gas."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why can the reading be unreliable in shock?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Peripheral vasoconstriction and poor perfusion reduce the pulsatile signal detected by the sensor."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What should you do if SpO₂ is 82% in a breathless patient?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess airway, breathing and circulation immediately, provide appropriate emergency support and oxygen according to the clinical situation, and escalate urgently. Check the probe and signal in parallel without delaying treatment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why might a severely anaemic patient have a normal SpO₂?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The device measures the percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen, not the total haemoglobin concentration or total oxygen content."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
